# Breakout Scanner — Docker Cron Schedule
# Managed by supercronic (runs as appuser, inherits all Docker env vars)
# Times are US Eastern (TZ set in Dockerfile ENV).
#
# Secrets are read from environment variables (pass via --env-file .env or compose.yaml):
#   HC_UUID_LONGTERM, HC_UUID_SWING, HC_UUID_SWING_CLOSE, HC_UUID_DAYTRADE,
#   HC_UUID_MONITOR, HC_UUID_MONITOR_OPEN, HC_UUID_MONITOR_CLOSE,
#   HC_UUID_VALIDATE, HC_UUID_VALIDATE_LEARN
# Healthcheck pings are skipped silently when UUIDs are not set.
#
# PING SHAPE — `cmd >> log 2>&1; curl ".../${UUID}/$?"` (note the `;`, not `&&`).
# The trailing /$? sends the job's exit status: /0 = success, non-zero = failure,
# so Healthchecks.io flags a dead job immediately instead of waiting out the grace
# window. Do NOT revert this to `cmd && curl ... || true`: with `&&` a non-zero exit
# short-circuits the curl so NO ping is ever sent, and the `|| true` then returns 0
# so supercronic logs "job succeeded". That combination hid four cgroup OOM kills on
# 2026-07-27 (swing-close among them) — the job was SIGKILLed at 1 GiB, never pinged,
# and only surfaced when Healthchecks' grace expired hours later. See CLAUDE.md §15.
# NOTE: SWING and VALIDATE were originally one UUID each covering two differently
# -timed cron lines (9:35am+4:30pm; weekday 8:30pm+Sunday 9pm). Split into
# _CLOSE / _LEARN so each Healthchecks.io monitor has ONE unambiguous expected
# schedule — a shared UUID across two different times can't be expressed without
# either false "missed ping" alerts or a grace window loose enough to miss a real
# outage. See CLAUDE.md section 9 (2026-07-23 reliability pass).
#
# MONITOR hit the same bug a third time (found 2026-08-05, CLAUDE.md §24): its
# single UUID fed THREE differently-timed lines (one ping at 9:45, a 15-min
# block 10:00-15:45, one ping at 16:00). No single 5-field cron expression can
# say "boundary hours get one minute, middle hours get four" — HC's schedule
# expected phantom pings at 9:00/9:15/9:30 and 16:15/16:30/16:45 that no line
# ever sends, so it went down every day after the last real ping. Split into
# _OPEN / (bare, now intraday-only) / _CLOSE, same fix as SWING/VALIDATE above.
#
# SERIALIZATION — every wide/re-evaluation scan runs under
#   flock -w 5400 /tmp/sb-heavy-scan.lock
# so that at most ONE of them is resident at a time. This is a MEMORY control, not
# a correctness one. Measured 2026-07-28 inside this container: FinBERT costs a
# FLAT ~704 MiB the moment the model loads (12 MiB baseline -> 115 on import ->
# 704 on first inference), and scoring 1 headline or 64 costs exactly the same.
# So the container's peak is (704 MiB x number of scans running concurrently),
# and it was the CONCURRENCY that OOM-killed us, not any single job:
#   - swing and daytrade both fired at 9:35 and each ran >60 min on all.txt
#     (1375 symbols), so they overlapped for their whole runtime;
#   - on Mondays the 9:00 longterm scan was STILL RUNNING at 10:00, making three
#     concurrent wide scans in one 1024 MiB cgroup.
# All three wide-scan logs ended in `Killed`. With the lock, peak is max(job)
# rather than sum(jobs). Do NOT put this lock on the exit / refresh_prices /
# monitor jobs: those are time-critical (a stop breach must not wait behind a
# 60-minute discovery scan) and are cheap enough not to need it.
# -w 5400 = wait up to 90 min, then give up and exit non-zero, which the /$? ping
# reports as a failure instead of hiding it. See CLAUDE.md section 15.
#
# Format: minute hour day month weekday command


# ============================================================================
# LONG-TERM POSITION TRADING (Weekly scans)
# ============================================================================

# Monday 6:00 AM — Phase 1: full scan, export PREMIUM tickers
# Moved 9:00 -> 6:00 (2026-07-28). On all.txt this job runs >60 min, so starting it
# at 9:00 left it still resident when the 9:35 swing scan launched — the Monday
# three-way overlap described in the SERIALIZATION note above. 6:00 gives it 3.5 h
# of clear air before 9:35, so the flock below should never actually have to block.
# Behaviourally identical: longterm works off daily bars, and both 6:00 and 9:00 are
# pre-open, so the last COMPLETE bar is the prior close either way.
# ⚠ The HC_UUID_LONGTERM monitor's expected schedule must be updated in the
#   Healthchecks.io dashboard to match, or it will report a missed ping every Monday.
0 6 * * 1 cd /app && flock -w 5400 /tmp/sb-heavy-scan.lock python3 breakout_scanner.py "input/all.txt" --mode longterm --bounce --sector-buzz --sentiment --export-premium input/premium_longterm.txt --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_longterm.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_LONGTERM}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# Monday 9:15 AM — Long-term exit evaluation
15 9 * * 1 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py input/1_26_Setups.txt --mode longterm --exit-file input/positions_longterm.csv --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_longterm_exit.log 2>&1


# ============================================================================
# SWING TRADING (Daily scans)
# ============================================================================

# Weekdays 9:35 AM — Phase 1: S&P 500 wide scan, export PREMIUM + auto-add positions
35 9 * * 1-5 cd /app && flock -w 5400 /tmp/sb-heavy-scan.lock python3 breakout_scanner.py "input/all.txt" --mode swing --bounce --sector-buzz --sentiment --auto-positions input/positions_swing_mock.csv --export-premium input/premium_swing.txt --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_swing.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_SWING}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# ── POSITION MANAGEMENT (refresh_prices) ───────────────────────────────────
# refresh_prices() is the ONLY code path that raises the ATR×2.0 trail and
# auto-closes a position on a stop breach. It was chained onto these same exit
# jobs in cron_jobs.txt (the retired Mac schedule) but never carried across when
# docker/crontab was branched — the same cron_jobs.txt-vs-docker/crontab drift
# already documented in CLAUDE.md §13 for the exit + monitor paths, third
# instance. Symptom (found 2026-07-27): every position in every book had
# current_price == entry_price, zero positions had ever been closed, and three
# sat 27–30% BELOW their stops since April.
#
# Two runs, matching the close-basis design in §12 Task 1 (dc3e252):
#   10:00 ET — pre-15:30, so _close_basis_history() drops today's partial bar and
#              decides on the last COMPLETED daily close (overnight-gap catch-up).
#   15:45 ET — late window, near-close price is a fair proxy for today's close.
# Uses *_all_users: plain refresh_prices() only touches the default book.
# _save() writes S3 via utils.save_json, so no separate upload step is needed.

# Weekdays 10:00 AM — Close-basis catch-up: raise trails, honour stops breached
# on yesterday's close.
0 10 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 -c "from auto_portfolio import refresh_prices_all_users; refresh_prices_all_users()" >> scanner_output/logs/cron_refresh.log 2>&1

# Weekdays 3:45 PM — Swing exit evaluation (from actual portfolio, not mock file)
# then refresh prices / raise trails / close stopped-out positions.
45 15 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py input/1_26_Setups.txt --mode swing --exit-from-portfolio --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_swing_exit.log 2>&1 && python3 -c "from auto_portfolio import refresh_prices_all_users; refresh_prices_all_users()" >> scanner_output/logs/cron_refresh.log 2>&1

# Weekdays 4:30 PM — Phase 2: Re-evaluate PREMIUM + combined exit check (use actual portfolio for exits)
30 16 * * 1-5 cd /app && flock -w 5400 /tmp/sb-heavy-scan.lock python3 breakout_scanner.py input/premium_swing.txt --mode swing --bounce --exit-from-portfolio --both --sentiment --auto-positions input/positions_swing_mock.csv --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_swing_combined.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_SWING_CLOSE}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# Weekdays 7:30 PM — Evening PREMIUM re-evaluation
30 19 * * 1-5 cd /app && flock -w 5400 /tmp/sb-heavy-scan.lock python3 breakout_scanner.py input/premium_swing.txt --mode swing --bounce --sentiment --auto-positions input/positions_swing_mock.csv --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_swing_evening.log 2>&1


# ============================================================================
# DAY TRADING (Intraday scans) — ⛔ RETIRED 2026-07-28
# ============================================================================
# Daytrade mode was declared out of use in CLAUDE.md section 9 ("didn't deliver good
# results; retiring its cron jobs is a pending follow-up, not yet done"), and
# HC_UUID_DAYTRADE was deliberately left unconfigured for the same reason. The jobs
# below were nevertheless still firing, and they were the single largest driver of
# the OOM kills: the 9:35 line scanned the same 1375-symbol all.txt as the swing
# scan, at the same minute, for >60 min, each holding its own flat 704 MiB FinBERT
# copy in a 1024 MiB cgroup.
#
# Trading impact is not merely "none because it's retired" — these lines DID reach
# the live book, because `--cron` makes breakout_scanner call
# scan_and_add_all_users(), which admits any GOLD/PREMIUM signal regardless of mode.
# The project's own A/B (CLAUDE.md section 7, daytrade_admission_ab.py) measured
# removing daytrade at B-A = +0.09 Sharpe — i.e. slightly FAVOURABLE, just under the
# +0.10 ship bar — and found only 2 of 41 control-arm trades were daytrade at all.
# So this is a small, measured, positive-signed change, not an unmeasured one.
#
# To restore: uncomment, re-add flock (see SERIALIZATION note) and set a
# non-overlapping start time, configure HC_UUID_DAYTRADE, and re-enable the
# momentum-watch monitor block below.
#
# 35 9 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py "input/all.txt" --mode daytrade --bounce --sector-buzz --sentiment --auto-positions input/positions_daytrade_mock.csv --export-premium input/premium_daytrade.txt --export-momentum-watch input/momentum_watch_daytrade.txt --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_daytrade_morning.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_DAYTRADE}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
# 0 10 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py input/momentum_watch_daytrade.txt --mode daytrade --bounce --sentiment --auto-positions input/positions_daytrade_mock.csv --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_daytrade_mid.log 2>&1
# 0 14 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py input/momentum_watch_daytrade.txt --mode daytrade --bounce --sentiment --auto-positions input/positions_daytrade_mock.csv --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_daytrade_afternoon.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_DAYTRADE}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
# 30 15 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py input/1_26_Setups.txt --mode daytrade --exit-from-portfolio --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_daytrade_exit.log 2>&1
# 20 20 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py input/momentum_watch_daytrade.txt --mode daytrade --bounce --sentiment --auto-positions input/positions_daytrade_mock.csv --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_daytrade_evening.log 2>&1


# ============================================================================
# MOMENTUM-WATCH MONITOR (Every 15 min) — ⛔ RETIRED 2026-07-28, with daytrade
# ============================================================================
# Validates signaled stocks: HOLDING / FADING / FAILED; Discord alert on first
# status change per day.
#
# Retired as a DIRECT CONSEQUENCE of retiring daytrade above, not as a separate
# decision. monitor_watch.py defaults to `--mode daytrade` (monitor_watch.py:70) and
# these three lines pass no --mode, so all of them read
# `scanner_output/lists/momentum_watch_daytrade.txt` — a file whose only writer was
# the daytrade 9:35 job's --export-momentum-watch. Leaving them enabled would have
# re-created CLAUDE.md section 13's exact bug: a never-refreshed list re-alerting on
# stale tickers every 15 minutes forever, with `.exit_history`-style per-day dedup
# being the only thing keeping the noise down.
#
# Switching them to `--mode swing` is NOT a drop-in fix: that reads
# `input/momentum_watch_swing.txt`, and no job exports it (the swing 9:35 line has no
# --export-momentum-watch). Wiring that up is the follow-up if this monitor is wanted
# back independently of daytrade.
#
# 45 9 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 monitor_watch.py --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_watch_monitor.log 2>&1
# */15 10-15 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 monitor_watch.py --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_watch_monitor.log 2>&1
# 0 16 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 monitor_watch.py --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_watch_monitor.log 2>&1


# ============================================================================
# PORTFOLIO MONITORING (Every 15 minutes during market hours)
# ============================================================================
# Price checks on open positions — alerts on drops toward stop loss.
# Source is portfolio.json + auto_portfolio.json (NOT positions_*_mock.csv).
# The mock CSVs are append-only and never pruned on exit, so monitoring them
# re-alerted on long-closed positions every 15 min. See CLAUDE.md section 13.

# Weekdays 9:30 AM — Roll over daily alert log
30 9 * * 1-5 mv /app/scanner_output/.monitor_alerts.txt /app/scanner_output/.monitor_alerts_$(date +%Y%m%d).txt 2>/dev/null || true

# Weekdays 9:45 AM — First check after open
45 9 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py /dev/null --monitor-portfolio --monitor-auto-portfolio --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_monitor.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_MONITOR_OPEN}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# Weekdays 10:00 AM – 3:45 PM — Continuous monitoring every 15 minutes
*/15 10-15 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py /dev/null --monitor-portfolio --monitor-auto-portfolio --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_monitor.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_MONITOR}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# Weekdays 4:00 PM — Final check at close
0 16 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 breakout_scanner.py /dev/null --monitor-portfolio --monitor-auto-portfolio --live --cron --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_monitor.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_MONITOR_CLOSE}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true


# ============================================================================
# REAL-TIME SURGE MONITOR (long-running, market hours only)
# ============================================================================
# Started once at 8:00 AM ET on weekdays; the script self-terminates at 4:15 PM
# ET (see its market-hours gating), so supercronic runs it as a single long job
# with no overlap or restart-loop. Watches premium/momentum lists for surges.

0 8 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 signal_surge_monitor.py --notify >> scanner_output/logs/cron_surge_monitor.log 2>&1


# ============================================================================
# SIGNAL VALIDATION & LEARNING
# ============================================================================

# Weekdays 8:30 PM — Validate signals from 3+ days ago
30 20 * * 1-5 cd /app && python3 validate_signals.py validate-all --min-age-days 3 >> scanner_output/logs/cron_validate.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_VALIDATE}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true

# Sundays 9:00 PM — Generate learning recommendations
0 21 * * 0 cd /app && python3 validate_signals.py learn >> scanner_output/logs/cron_learn.log 2>&1; curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 "https://hc-ping.com/${HC_UUID_VALIDATE_LEARN}/$?" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true


# ============================================================================
# MAINTENANCE
# ============================================================================

# Sundays 11:00 PM — Prune stale scanner_output files (cleanup_outputs.py)
#
# Replaces the previous ad-hoc `find -mtime -delete` lines, which had a hole:
# the per-job logs (cron_swing.log, cron_monitor.log, …) are APPENDED to on
# every run, so their mtime is always today and `-mtime +30` never matched them.
# They grew without bound and were never rotated. cleanup_outputs.py rotates any
# log over 5 MB (truncate, keep last 500 lines) and applies per-type retention:
#   logs 7d · rejections 14d · exits/signals/signal_reports 30d · outcomes 60d
#   scan_feedback 90d · state JSONs + monitor/watch files 7–14d
# Note this is stricter than the finds it replaces (signals/exits 30d, was 90d).
# Preview any time with: docker compose exec scanner-cron python3 cleanup_outputs.py --dry-run
0 23 * * 0 cd /app && python3 cleanup_outputs.py >> scanner_output/logs/cron_cleanup.log 2>&1

# NOTE — Docker build cache is NOT covered here and cannot be: this container has
# no access to the Docker socket. It is by far the largest consumer on this box
# (11.89 GB on 2026-07-23, which filled the 29 GB disk and failed a deploy with
# "no space left on device"). It is pruned by a HOST cron entry running
# `docker builder prune -af`. See CLAUDE.md section 13.


# ============================================================================
# TRADE JOURNAL — SUPABASE KEEP-ALIVE
# ============================================================================
# Free-tier Supabase pauses projects after ~1 week without API activity, which
# takes journal.gilhadas-stocks.com down until manually restored. One cheap
# authenticated REST query per day counts as activity and prevents the pause.
# Skips silently when the VITE_SUPABASE_* vars are not set in .env.

# Daily 6:00 AM — ping the trades endpoint (RLS returns an empty list; that's fine)
0 6 * * * [ -n "${VITE_SUPABASE_URL}" ] && curl -fsS -m 10 --retry 3 -H "apikey: ${VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY}" -H "Authorization: Bearer ${VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY}" "${VITE_SUPABASE_URL}/rest/v1/trades?select=id&limit=1" > /dev/null 2>&1 || true
